error[ffi_writable_parameter]: `s` of `va_scribble` is declared `cstr lent`, and `fixedbugs-2102-variadic.h` gives `va_scribble` 1 parameter and then `...`, where `s` stands — C reads what a call hands it there as whatever type it asks for, and does not promise to leave it alone
  at tests/golden/unsupported/fixedbugs-2102-a-read-only-lend-past-a-variadic-prototype-is-refused.hero:25:34
     |
  25 |     function va_scribble(k: i32, s: cstr lent) -> i64  #~ ffi_writable_parameter
     |                                  ^
  note: spec § 13: `s.cstr()` lends a `str` to C, and C writes back through a lend only where the binding is a `@` name. C11 6.5.2.2p7: past a prototype's `...` a call converts nothing beyond the default argument promotions, and `va_scribble` reads the pointer as the type its `va_arg` names, so neither clang nor this compiler can hold C to the `const`: a write there changes the bytes of every value that shares the string, and on a literal's stops the program
  note: write a header of your own that includes `fixedbugs-2102-variadic.h` and defines a `static inline` function of a new name with a fixed prototype, taking `s` as the `const char *` `va_scribble` reads there and calling `va_scribble` with it; bind that function instead, and its prototype holds C to the `const`; where `va_scribble` reads a format, write the format inside that function as a literal, and clang reads each conversion against the argument it is given
